WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:05.429
Next up, let's have a look at Overlaying Materials.

2
00:00:05.429 --> 00:00:09.070
Now, this is new in Unreal Engine version 5,

3
00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:11.349
so you might not have seen this before,

4
00:00:11.349 --> 00:00:15.710
but it really is a great way of doing a lot of things,

5
00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:18.149
which originally was quite hard to do.

6
00:00:18.149 --> 00:00:20.829
You've seen how we needed to create

7
00:00:20.829 --> 00:00:25.429
dynamic material instances in order to create effects,

8
00:00:25.429 --> 00:00:27.910
or we could use the Post-Process Volume.

9
00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:30.230
In this, we created

10
00:00:30.289 --> 00:00:32.369
these effects that you can see here,

11
00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:36.170
which is like a track and checker pattern.

12
00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:39.250
It works on skeletal meshes, standard meshes,

13
00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:42.049
can even function as an X-ray material.

14
00:00:42.049 --> 00:00:44.969
I'll show you how to do that in a little bit.

15
00:00:44.969 --> 00:00:47.969
But the great thing about this is it's the same material

16
00:00:47.969 --> 00:00:52.090
and you can see that the material below it

17
00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:53.950
is not interfered with.

18
00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:58.950
So this can be applied on any specific mesh

19
00:00:59.609 --> 00:01:03.210
and you'll still be able to see the material below.

20
00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:06.569
Now, this is useful because if you were to program

21
00:01:06.569 --> 00:01:10.189
all of your effects into a material,

22
00:01:10.189 --> 00:01:14.010
you'd have to write it into the master material

23
00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:15.930
and the material that it came with.

24
00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:19.290
And this could make it, firstly, very, very complicated.

25
00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:20.969
It means that a lot of people

26
00:01:20.969 --> 00:01:22.930
need to work on one material

27
00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:25.950
and there might be a bunch of efficiency concerns.

28
00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:28.090
So achieving something like this,

29
00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:32.189
especially if you have some areas of transparency and stuff,

30
00:01:32.189 --> 00:01:37.109
this is a lot easier by using these overlay materials.

31
00:01:37.109 --> 00:01:39.870
Now, they can be simpler to use.

32
00:01:41.349 --> 00:01:43.109
If you look at a mesh,

33
00:01:43.109 --> 00:01:46.950
you'll see that we have our standard material slot,

34
00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:51.189
but we also have a overlay materials slot.

35
00:01:51.189 --> 00:01:54.170
Let me just search for it here.

36
00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:55.650
Overlay materials.

37
00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:57.430
And if I was to remove this,

38
00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.569
it would just go back to being a normal overlay material.

39
00:02:00.569 --> 00:02:04.489
And if I place the material in, it will affect it.

40
00:02:04.489 --> 00:02:07.189
Even with this skeletal mesh,

41
00:02:07.189 --> 00:02:09.210
even though this has two material slots,

42
00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:12.770
it still only has one material slot for the overlay.

43
00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:16.449
And that's really quick and straightforward to use.

44
00:02:16.449 --> 00:02:18.889
So another thing that's usefully built in here,

45
00:02:18.889 --> 00:02:23.330
we can set the overlay material max draw distance.

46
00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:25.370
So we can have this material turn off.

47
00:02:25.389 --> 00:02:30.300
So if I was to set this to just, let's say 1,000,

48
00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:35.009
and as I move further away,

49
00:02:35.009 --> 00:02:37.530
we should see that that stops displaying.

50
00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:39.330
And you can only do that when you're in game mode.

51
00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:42.710
So you have to push G in order to see this,

52
00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:44.250
and see the material just switches off

53
00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:45.689
at a certain distance.

54
00:02:45.689 --> 00:02:47.969
And that's really useful as an optimization,

55
00:02:47.969 --> 00:02:50.710
especially if one of something like this is very complicated

56
00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:51.969
and you don't want to calculate it

57
00:02:51.969 --> 00:02:56.969
when it's a small three sprites tall in the distance.

58
00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:01.689
So that's how you would add an overlay material on.

59
00:03:02.689 --> 00:03:04.650
Let's look at the creation of one.

60
00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:08.349
So next up, I'm going to go into exercise four here.

61
00:03:08.349 --> 00:03:11.030
And we can see that it will highlight a material

62
00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:14.680
that's called M underscore overlay.

63
00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:17.580
So what I'd like you to do is to open this up

64
00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:21.340
and you should get a material which looks like this.

65
00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:25.580
Now we're going to recreate that checkered pattern one

66
00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:26.960
quite quickly.

67
00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:31.500
So what we've got here is we can use the surface.

68
00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:33.139
We could use other methods as well,

69
00:03:33.139 --> 00:03:35.379
but we're going to go with surface for this one.

70
00:03:35.379 --> 00:03:37.620
And first thing that I want to do

71
00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:39.099
is set an emissive color.

72
00:03:39.099 --> 00:03:40.620
So I want this to go up.

73
00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:42.840
So I'm going to hold down three,

74
00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:47.400
plug this in, and I'll make this one red.

75
00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:52.449
So I'll make the color that we're doing this in red.

76
00:03:52.449 --> 00:03:56.960
Now, next up, I'm going to use a texture top.

77
00:03:57.419 --> 00:03:59.539
So if I hold down T and left-click,

78
00:03:59.539 --> 00:04:01.860
that will give me a texture sample.

79
00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:05.500
And in the dropdown at the bottom here,

80
00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:08.650
I'm going to search for checkers.

81
00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:12.810
You can see that we have a checkered noise pattern here

82
00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:15.009
and I'll use that.

83
00:04:15.009 --> 00:04:19.930
And I know, I think we can use green in this instance

84
00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:24.180
and plug that into the opacity mask.

85
00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:28.519
You can see that suddenly our sphere or cube

86
00:04:28.519 --> 00:04:32.519
is now a lot of it is opaque.

87
00:04:32.519 --> 00:04:35.579
Now that works fine, but I want to do some extra stuff

88
00:04:35.579 --> 00:04:37.540
to make this look better.

89
00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:39.660
So the first thing I'm going to do

90
00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.629
is I'm going to use a pan.

91
00:04:41.629 --> 00:04:43.550
So if I hold down P and left-click,

92
00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:45.170
that's going to give me a panino.

93
00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:48.490
I'll plug that into the UVs.

94
00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:53.490
Now, this is going to move the texture across the UVs.

95
00:04:53.990 --> 00:04:58.829
What I've opted for is to just put one in both the U,

96
00:04:58.829 --> 00:05:00.829
the X and the Y axis.

97
00:05:00.829 --> 00:05:05.829
You can now see that it is moving here up and to the left.

98
00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:09.839
Now this is too large for me.

99
00:05:09.839 --> 00:05:15.089
So I'm going to want to shrink this texture down.

100
00:05:15.449 --> 00:05:17.569
And the way that we would do that is,

101
00:05:17.569 --> 00:05:20.230
the first thing is we would need the texture UVs

102
00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:21.069
for the model.

103
00:05:21.069 --> 00:05:23.970
So if you hold down the U key and left-click,

104
00:05:24.269 --> 00:05:26.579
that's going to give you the texture coordinates.

105
00:05:26.579 --> 00:05:29.420
And then if I hold down M and left-click,

106
00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:31.680
that's going to give us a multiply.

107
00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:38.279
If I plug these in, we'll just multiply this by 10.

108
00:05:38.399 --> 00:05:40.519
That will make this 10 times smaller

109
00:05:40.519 --> 00:05:42.740
and it'll move a lot slower.

110
00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:45.230
So that works quite well.

111
00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:49.060
Okay, so this is the okay material.

112
00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:50.620
There's one more thing that I'd like to do

113
00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:51.740
before we leave this,

114
00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:54.810
and that's the world position offset.

115
00:05:54.829 --> 00:05:57.750
Now, one of the problems that you might get

116
00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:00.430
when you're working with overlay materials

117
00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:03.910
is that it would clash with the model

118
00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:05.790
that's already there.

119
00:06:05.790 --> 00:06:09.990
So because it might be fighting for the same location,

120
00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:13.750
there might be a little bit of sort of cutting in and out

121
00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:16.339
and it wouldn't look how you want it to look.

122
00:06:16.339 --> 00:06:19.560
Now, what I would suggest is if you want to,

123
00:06:20.519 --> 00:06:23.319
is to just push the UVs out a little bit

124
00:06:23.339 --> 00:06:26.889
by using the world position offset.

125
00:06:26.889 --> 00:06:30.149
So in order to do this, what I would do is right-click

126
00:06:30.149 --> 00:06:35.149
and I would search for Vertex Normals World Space, WS.

127
00:06:38.079 --> 00:06:42.420
And then hold down M for a multiply.

128
00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:47.589
I'm going to multiply it just by about 0.3

129
00:06:47.589 --> 00:06:49.750
and plug that into the world position offset.

130
00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:50.709
So all that's going to do

131
00:06:50.709 --> 00:06:53.189
is it's going to push all of the vertices out

132
00:06:53.209 --> 00:06:58.209
just a small amount on the normals of the vertices,

133
00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:02.600
just so we make sure we don't get a clash.

134
00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:06.899
I'll apply and save.

135
00:07:06.899 --> 00:07:10.100
And now if I jump back into the level,

136
00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:17.139
if I search for these, I'm typing overlay,

137
00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:20.560
I'm going to just drag my overlay material in.

138
00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:24.339
And you can see that on all of these meshes,

139
00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:28.639
it will show through, it will automatically be updated.

140
00:07:28.639 --> 00:07:29.759
Now there's a couple of other things

141
00:07:29.759 --> 00:07:31.839
you might want to do at this point.

142
00:07:31.839 --> 00:07:35.759
You can hype up, we can multiply up the emissive possibly

143
00:07:35.759 --> 00:07:40.819
so that it is a lot brighter.

144
00:07:42.079 --> 00:07:45.579
Can change the shading model so that it is,

145
00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:50.899
change the shading model so that it is additive

146
00:07:50.899 --> 00:07:52.579
under the blend mode.

147
00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:54.339
It's because we're now not plugged in

148
00:07:54.339 --> 00:07:57.120
the opacity correctly here.

149
00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:00.879
And that just gives us a bit of a more of a glowing effect

150
00:08:00.879 --> 00:08:07.899
and it's not terribly expensive.

151
00:08:08.259 --> 00:08:12.319
And so that's one option that we have there.

152
00:08:12.319 --> 00:08:15.709
Now, how do we get this to show through?

153
00:08:15.709 --> 00:08:19.149
Like with an X-ray before,

154
00:08:19.149 --> 00:08:22.310
we can get this to show through what's in front of it.

155
00:08:22.329 --> 00:08:26.129
And how we would do that is if we've actually come

156
00:08:26.129 --> 00:08:28.170
into the material again,

157
00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:33.429
I'm going to change the blending mode to translucent.

158
00:08:33.429 --> 00:08:37.090
And as soon as we're in translucent,

159
00:08:37.090 --> 00:08:39.789
there is, if we type in depth,

160
00:08:39.789 --> 00:08:44.840
we have an option to disable the depth test.

161
00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:49.340
And what that is, is we have occlusion culling,

162
00:08:49.340 --> 00:08:51.259
which is built into the engine.

163
00:08:51.259 --> 00:08:53.059
And that means it won't draw a pixel

164
00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:55.519
when there's something else in front of it.

165
00:08:55.519 --> 00:08:58.059
Now, if we disable this depth test,

166
00:08:58.059 --> 00:09:00.159
it's never gonna know if it's in front

167
00:09:00.159 --> 00:09:01.000
or behind something.

168
00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:02.879
So we will draw it regardless.

169
00:09:02.879 --> 00:09:05.039
It needs to be translucent.

170
00:09:05.039 --> 00:09:07.159
You'll now see that all of these models

171
00:09:07.159 --> 00:09:10.190
now show through the background.

172
00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:12.590
So that's just a short exercise showing you

173
00:09:12.590 --> 00:09:17.590
how you can create an overlay material and apply it.

174
00:09:18.669 --> 00:09:22.539
I'm just going to undisable that simply because

175
00:09:22.559 --> 00:09:24.139
I don't want to be seeing that in the corner

176
00:09:24.139 --> 00:09:26.649
every time I move around the world.

177
00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:29.809
But let's have a look at some examples.

178
00:09:29.809 --> 00:09:33.129
So I'm now going to come on to slide 13.

179
00:09:33.129 --> 00:09:35.250
Let's have a look at how you might use

180
00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:38.250
or implement this within the game.

181
00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:40.789
So now we've seen these before,

182
00:09:40.789 --> 00:09:43.889
these simple sort of balls on a pedestal.

183
00:09:43.889 --> 00:09:48.649
And what they do is when you get close enough to them,

184
00:09:48.649 --> 00:09:52.750
they cast to the third person character

185
00:09:52.750 --> 00:09:54.809
or check it's a third person character.

186
00:09:54.809 --> 00:09:58.129
And if it is, it will set the overlay material

187
00:09:58.129 --> 00:10:01.450
on the target, on the skeleton mesh.

188
00:10:01.450 --> 00:10:04.490
So it's as straightforward as this.

189
00:10:04.490 --> 00:10:08.730
All you need to do is set a new overlay material

190
00:10:08.730 --> 00:10:11.529
or remove the overlay material.

191
00:10:11.529 --> 00:10:16.750
So if you wanted to unmesh, you could set overlay,

192
00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:22.580
set the overlay material to nothing and it would go away.

193
00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:26.440
So this will allow you to turn on and off the materials

194
00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:27.799
or apply it to anything.

195
00:10:27.799 --> 00:10:29.559
It's the same method regardless.

196
00:10:29.559 --> 00:10:32.080
It just has to be onto the mesh.

197
00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:35.139
So let's demonstrate this.

198
00:10:35.139 --> 00:10:37.799
So all there are is just a couple of different effects

199
00:10:37.799 --> 00:10:39.799
that you can check out.

200
00:10:39.799 --> 00:10:42.700
So the first one is an overlay,

201
00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:47.700
which is demonstrating the outline.

202
00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:51.059
Now this is done by pushing it backwards

203
00:10:51.059 --> 00:10:53.559
and using custom depth.

204
00:10:53.559 --> 00:10:55.240
It's a bit jittery as you move,

205
00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:59.360
but when you're still, it looks very good.

206
00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:04.480
Next one up is a kind of a sort of force field,

207
00:11:06.549 --> 00:11:24.440
some electrical bolts, fire, ice, and some tar.

208
00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:28.289
So you can see how these kinds of things can be applied

209
00:11:28.289 --> 00:11:29.789
and they can be applied to any meshes.

210
00:11:29.789 --> 00:11:32.250
They can be guns, skeletal meshes, anything.

211
00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:34.330
But it's very, very quick to get them on

212
00:11:34.389 --> 00:11:36.730
and very easy to remove.

213
00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:39.269
Only one can ever be applied at one time.
